# Pin CRLF for addon source shipped to Windows WoW clients (F-012 hardening).
# Lua/XML/TOC are checked out with CRLF regardless of the committer's platform.
*.lua text eol=crlf
*.xml text eol=crlf
*.toc text eol=crlf

# Vendored libraries are committed verbatim (Ka0s Standard v1.1). Leave their bytes
# exactly as committed — no eol normalization — so they never show as renormalized
# changes. This MUST come after the globs above so it wins for libs/.
libs/** -text

# tests/_kit/ is the same deal: a byte-for-byte vendored copy of ../LibKa0s/testkit.
# The gate that keeps it honest is `diff -r ../LibKa0s/testkit tests/_kit`, which compares RAW
# BYTES, so letting the CRLF filter touch these files would break a check that is meant to catch a
# real fork. (testkit/README.md is CRLF in the library repo; every .lua there is too.)
tests/_kit/** -text

# docs/ is LF like the rest of this repo's Markdown, with ONE exception: docs/test-cases.md
# is generated by the shared LibKa0s test kit, whose renderer writes CRLF itself (the library
# repo pins `*.md text eol=crlf`; this one does not). Pinning it here keeps the stored form and
# what `lua tests/run.lua --list` writes in agreement, so regenerating never shows as a
# whole-file diff. Do not "fix" it with a `| sed 's/$/\r/'` pipeline — a regeneration command
# with a pipeline in it is one someone eventually runs without the pipeline.
docs/test-cases.md text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are LF, ALWAYS — even here, where everything else is CRLF.
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash` followed by CRLF makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# literally named "bash\r", and every `case`/`in` line becomes a syntax error.
# The vendored tests/_kit/run-automated-tests.sh is the file this protects
# (automated-tests-§2); without it the runner is broken on every checkout.
*.sh   text eol=lf
